          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Dold–Kan correspondence
            asserts there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+groups"} simplicial groups
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connective+chain+complex"} connective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
          %p
            Since every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
            is in particular a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            with group structure, hence an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group"} ∞-group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connective+chain+complexes"} connective chain complexes
            are a model for certain
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+%E2%88%9E-groups"} abelian ∞-groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            and general
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+theory"} simplicial homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+cube"} cosmic cube
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            apply. In fact, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            that identifies simplicial abelian groups with their corresponding chain complexes – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} normalized chains functor
            – does precisely this: it
            %em normalizes
            an abelian group by discarding irrelevant information and constructing a smaller and less redundant model for it.
          %p
            There are various variants and generalizations of the Dold-Kan correspondence. These are discussed further
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Variants"} below
          %h2#statement_abelian_case Statement (abelian case)
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            (or more generally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+category"} additive category
          %p
            We say a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is
            %em connective
            if it is concentrated in non-negative degree. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ch^+_\bullet(A) \hookrightarrow Ch_\bullet(A)
          %p
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connective+chain+complexes"} connective chain complexes
            is naturally identified with the category of
            = succeed "-graded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          %h3#EquivalenceOfCategories Equivalence of categories
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Dold–Puppe)
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
              there is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi A
